WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] You know that
frustrating feeling when

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.050
a message takes more than
a few seconds to send?

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.270
Well, it's hard to imagine today,

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.400
but before the mid-19th century,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.100
sending news across the ocean

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.903
could take weeks to arrive.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.770
Then, thanks to an incredible,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.550
yet mostly forgotten
technological achievement,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.730
that gap was shrunk from weeks to seconds.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:24.510
A feat that kicked off the global age

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.310
of instant communication that
we take for granted today.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.440
For most of human history,

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.220
the only way to send a
message across a long distance

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.200
was to have it delivered in person.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.480
Which was limited by how
fast someone could travel.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.210
The invention of the electric telegraph

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.180
in the 1830s sparked a revolution.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.040
It allowed messages to be sent across

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.470
wires as electrical pulses which could

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:50.340
be translated into letters and words.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.980
Within a decade of its invention,

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:54.760
thousands of miles of
telegraph cables were installed

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.870
between major cities
worldwide linking governments,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.690
businesses, individuals, and the press.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.410
But there was still an
enormous, daunting gap.

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.750
How to send a signal thousands of miles

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.840
across the vast Atlantic Ocean

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.340
linking Europe and the Americas?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.300
In 1854, an ambitious,
wealthy young American

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:16.000
entrepreneur named Cyrus West Field

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.490
was presented with a business opportunity,

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.900
to connect a telegraph
cable from New York City

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.390
to Newfoundland which
would shorten the arrival

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.973
of news by ship from
abroad by a day or two.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.170
Field pushed the idea even further.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:30.590
What if the line could be extended

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.320
all the way to Ireland,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.150
and then unto London which at the time

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.520
was the hub of the global economy?

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.630
Sensing that his project could
change the course of history,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.520
Field was undaunted by
the immense challenge.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.050
He raised money from a
wide network of investors,

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.400
including the British
and American governments,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.360
which also provided the necessary ships

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.350
for laying the cable in exchange for

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.010
top priority communication rights

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.760
on the future telegraph line.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.460
Field also recruited some of the top

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.670
engineers and scientists of his time

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.280
to develop a cable durable enough

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.573
to carry a signal thousands of
miles across the ocean floor.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.810
Laying out the actual
cable by steamer ship

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.130
in the rough waters of the North Atlantic

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.400
was no easy feat either, and in fact,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.647
the first two attempts failed when

00:02:12.647 --> 00:02:15.360
the cable snapped and sunk in the journey

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.110
wasting investor money, dashing hopes,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.480
and causing widespread public
mockery of the project.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.600
Some of the criticisms were philosophical

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.903
and eerily relevant today.

00:02:25.749 --> 00:02:27.590
Would instant access to information really

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.893
makes us any happier or better off?

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.480
Nevertheless, in August 1858,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.600
after over a year of disappointments,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.370
the two massive cable-laying ships

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.180
successfully reached their destinations

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.920
in Newfoundland and Ireland.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.510
Completing the connection that Field

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.230
and others had prophesied.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:45.750
Once the line had been tested,

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.160
an official message of goodwill

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.420
was exchanged between Queen Victoria

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.203
and U.S. President James Buchanan.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.030
Public celebrations erupted in sieges

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.270
throughout the U.S. and
Europe marking the dawn

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:58.330
of a new era of unification between

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.720
the Old World and the new.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.860
It's hard to overstate how influential

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.060
the so-called Electric Union was to

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.240
the rapidly globalizing
world of the 19th century.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.710
It meant that everything from stock

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.320
and commodity prices, to a
declaration of war or peace

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:15.440
could now be shared immediately.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.560
It's no surprise that
Field's accomplishment

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.370
was widely heralded at
the time as the greatest

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.940
human achievement in history,

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.623
and Field himself became
an international hero.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.010
Although the first cable only functioned

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:29.870
for a few months before going dead,

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.030
it proved that a link was indeed possible.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.030
Within a decade,

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.650
more reliable and sophisticated

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.883
undersea cables were laid
by Field and many others.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.940
Throughout the 20th century,
cable networks expanded,

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:44.800
and technology continued to evolve

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.490
as telephones eventually
replaced telegraphs,

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.423
and the digital data eventually
replaced analog signals.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.740
While the original
Transatlantic cable could

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.180
only transmit a few
words per minute at best,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.470
today's fiber optic
cables deliver hundreds

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.703
of terabytes of data in just seconds,

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.410
and there are a lot of them,

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.070
stretching close to
750,000 miles in total,

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.260
and more cable was laid
in 2018 than any year

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.270
in almost two decades.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.920
Instead of brave entrepreneurs,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.360
now most new cables are being
laid by the tech giants,

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.320
such as Amazon, Facebook,
Google, and Microsoft,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.110
which collectively own or lease more than

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.670
half of the undersea bandwidth.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:25.970
And although it seems that we live

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:28.460
in a mostly wireless world,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.270
undersea cable still carry 99% of data

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.690
traffic that crosses the oceans.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.150
So the next time you chat with someone

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.700
on the other side of the world,

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.070
keep in mind that it all
started over 150 years ago

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.420
by a visionary entrepreneur and his team,

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.760
pulling off what was once
called the greatest work

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.810
that the genius of man ever contemplated.

